# Live AI answers. Absent -> FakeProvider (offline dictionary mode).
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# fake | anthropic
# AnthropicProvider is selected only when this is `anthropic` AND a key is set;
# either half missing falls back to the fake (packages/ai/provider.ts).
TANGRAM_LLM_PROVIDER=fake
# Model id used by AnthropicProvider; empty -> claude-opus-5 (packages/ai/anthropic.ts,
# DEFAULT_MODEL). Use the bare id, never a date-suffixed variant.
TANGRAM_MODEL=
# Where dict.json / decomp.json live. Empty -> <repo>/data.
TANGRAM_DATA_DIR=

# --- Deployment (docs/deploy.md) ---
# Shared secret that gates the three routes which spend money on the model:
# /api/ask, /api/examples, /api/recall, AND every path under them — the match is
# by prefix (wave-zero.md §10a), which is what will cover backend.md B2's
# /api/ask/propose and /api/ask/answer. LEAVE IT EMPTY LOCALLY — with no value
# the gate does not exist and the app, the tests and `pnpm e2e` behave exactly
# as they always have.
#
# It belongs to **apps/server** (backend.md B1), never to the static app: the
# three handlers live there now and read it as the first line of each. Authorise
# a device once by visiting https://<app>/?key=<secret>; page script takes the
# key out of the URL and stores it in localStorage, then sends it as the
# X-Tangram-Access header on every call (web.md W4 — it was an HttpOnly cookie
# until then, and this line said so). URL-safe characters only
# ([A-Za-z0-9._~-]) — the value travels verbatim. Rotating it revokes every
# device at once, because there is no session store.
#   openssl rand -base64 24 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
TANGRAM_ACCESS_SECRET=

# --- The server, apps/server (docs/plans/backend.md B0) ---
# The port the SERVER listens on. Empty -> 8787.
#
# Deliberately NOT `PORT` here: `PORT` is already the app's, read by
# apps/app/playwright.config.ts, scripts/preview.ts and scripts/smoke.ts, and
# documented in CLAUDE.md as the e2e port (default 3000). Exporting PORT=8787
# from this file would move `pnpm e2e` onto the API server. The server still
# falls back to PORT when this is unset, so a host that injects only PORT works.
# An unparseable value is a hard failure at boot rather than a silent fall back:
# a service on the wrong port looks healthy locally and is unreachable in the
# deployment.
TANGRAM_SERVER_PORT=
# The interface to bind. Empty -> 0.0.0.0, which is what a container needs.
# Set 127.0.0.1 for local work; 0.0.0.0 on a laptop is every interface.
HOST=
# `development` or `test` enables developer conveniences — notably the error
# message in a 500 body. ANYTHING ELSE, INCLUDING UNSET, IS PRODUCTION, which is
# the safe direction: most hosts inject nothing, and an error from the driver or
# the model SDK routinely carries a connection target. TANGRAM_EXPOSE_ERRORS=1
# is the explicit opt-in when NODE_ENV is not yours to set.
NODE_ENV=
TANGRAM_EXPOSE_ERRORS=
# How long SIGTERM waits for in-flight requests before cutting them. Empty ->
# 35000, comfortably past the 30 s ask deadline. A forced exit logs and exits 75.
TANGRAM_DRAIN_MS=
# The git sha `/health` reports. Normally written into dist/build-info.json by
# `pnpm -F server build`; set this only on a host that builds from an archive
# with no .git of its own.
TANGRAM_BUILD_SHA=
# Comma-separated origins allowed to call this server from a browser
# (backend.md B1). Set it to the app's origin — `https://app.<domain>`.
#
# It is ADDITIVE, never a replacement: `capacitor://localhost` (iOS) and
# `http://localhost` (Android) are always allowed, because one build ships to
# three places and neither phone is on any domain. The development origins
# (localhost:3000, localhost:5173 and their 127.0.0.1 spellings) are allowed
# only when NODE_ENV says development — a production allowlist that admitted a
# developer's laptop would not be saying anything.
#
# CORS is NOT the gate. It is a rule a browser applies; curl and a native client
# ignore it. TANGRAM_ACCESS_SECRET is what stops a stranger spending money. What
# this does decide is whether the app can reach the API at all: a custom request
# header makes every cross-origin POST preflighted, so an origin missing from
# this list fails every call before the handler is reached.
TANGRAM_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# How long a provider may take, per route, in milliseconds. All four are
# overrides: empty means the default in apps/server/src/config.ts
# (DEADLINE_DEFAULTS), which is what the handlers have always used. They exist
# so a deployment behind a slower upstream — or behind a host whose function
# limit is under 30 s (backend.md §6) — can move them with no rebuild, and so a
# test can prove a deadline fires without waiting 30 s for it.
TANGRAM_ASK_PROPOSE_TIMEOUT_MS=
TANGRAM_ASK_ANSWER_TIMEOUT_MS=
TANGRAM_EXAMPLES_TIMEOUT_MS=
TANGRAM_RECALL_TIMEOUT_MS=
